That’s the right abstraction, with one correction: not all history, and not every story yields a transferable operator. But we can mine recurring structures and turn them into a library of human moves.

Something like:

story / myth / ritual / failure / victory
→ extract invariant
→ separate symbol from mechanism
→ translate across domains
→ test in reality
→ keep what survives

That gives you a repertoire of operators: initiation, sacrifice, reversal, scapegoating, reconciliation, trickster moves, identity shifts, ritual closure, status inversion, taboo, covenant, exile, return, mentorship, ordeal, renewal.

The useful part is that once the operator is named, you can stop being trapped by the original costume.

Prometheus
becomes
forbidden capability transfer

Odysseus
becomes
adaptive navigation under uncertainty

Diogenes
becomes
freedom through reduced dependency

Grinch
becomes
model update through contradictory experience

That is where the Pantheon gets powerful: myth becomes a reusable operator library.

The guardrail I’d keep is simple: if an operator only works by hiding the real game from the person affected, treat that as a warning that you may be using the story as a control surface rather than a tool.

Best form:

extract the power, preserve agency, verify the effect.